curl for OpenVMS (vms-curl)
===========================

This is curl @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS @ARCH@, from the
unmodified curl release plus the patches in the vms-curl project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-curl

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  curl is distributed under the curl licence
(MIT style); see COPYING.  The project follows curl's own releases, so the
kit is updated soon after each new curl version.

What is installed
-----------------
    VMSCURL$ROOT:[BIN]CURL.EXE      curl, with libcurl linked in
    VMSCURL$ROOT:[SSL]CACERT.PEM    the default CA certificates
    VMSCURL$ROOT:[000000]VMSCURL$SETUP.COM
                                    defines the curl command
    VMSCURL$ROOT:[DOC]              this file, COPYING, MANUAL.TXT,
                                    SSLCERTS.TXT, RELEASE-NOTES
    SYS$STARTUP:VMSCURL$STARTUP.COM defines VMSCURL$ROOT

TLS uses VSI's OpenSSL 3.0 kit (SSL3), a prerequisite of this kit; zlib
and zstd (for --compressed: gzip, deflate and zstd) are linked in.  LDAP uses the VMS LDAP library.  There
is no HTTP/2, HTTP/3, SSH (scp/sftp) or Kerberos support in this build.

Setting up
----------
The kit installs VMSCURL$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.
To define VMSCURL$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:VMSCURL$STARTUP.COM

Each user (or SYLOGIN.COM, for everyone) then defines the command:

    $ @VMSCURL$ROOT:[000000]VMSCURL$SETUP.COM

VSI's own CURL kit uses CURL$ROOT and [CURL]; this kit uses VMSCURL$ROOT
and [VMSCURL], so both can be installed.  VMSCURL$SETUP.COM makes "curl"
run this kit's CURL.EXE.

CA certificates
---------------
curl verifies servers against VMSCURL$ROOT:[SSL]CACERT.PEM by default:
Mozilla's root certificates as extracted by the curl project
(https://curl.se/docs/caextract.html), dated @CABUNDLEDATE@.  To use other
certificates, give --cacert file, or define the logical name
CURL_CA_BUNDLE to a PEM file.  Newer kits bring a newer bundle.

Upper-case options: the DCL parse style
---------------------------------------
Under the TRADITIONAL parse style DCL upper-cases the unquoted parts of a
command and the C run-time library passes them to curl in lower case, so
an unquoted -F arrives as -f (--fail) and -O as -o.  Batch jobs use
TRADITIONAL even when interactive logins are set to EXTENDED, so a
command that works interactively can fail in a batch job: a form post
typically ends with %CURL-E-URL_MALFORMAT, because the form field is
taken as a URL.  Either quote the options that use capital letters

    $ curl "https://example.com/post" "-F" "name=value"

or start the procedure with

    $ SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED

When curl fails under TRADITIONAL, it names the unquoted options that may
have lost their case.

Exit status
-----------
curl exits with a VMS condition value: success, or %CURL-E-<error> with
the curl error number in the message code (for example
%CURL-E-URL_MALFORMAT).  Test $STATUS or $SEVERITY in procedures.

Output
------
Output to the terminal, a batch log or a mailbox is written in records,
one per line.  Use -o file to save a response exactly as received.
